    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0f656c39-8514-5dee-ac94-b51bee6d60b0",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -6 & 40 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 28\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3 & -6 & 40 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 28\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & \\frac{40}{3} \\\\ 2 & -3 & 28\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & \\frac{40}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=\\frac{40}{3} \\\\ y &=\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=\\frac{4}{3}$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)=\\frac{40}{3}\\,\\Rightarrow\\,x=\\frac{40}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=16$ and $y=\\frac{4}{3}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
